# Quality attributes

Detail behind the catalog in SKILL.md. Open it while screening (to decide a verdict) and while
drafting (to pick the sub-characteristic and a measure). FUNC to SAFE are the nine product quality
characteristics of ISO/IEC 25010:2023 with the standard's sub-characteristic names; the meanings
are short paraphrases, not the standard's wording. The other codes are architecture concerns the
standard does not name. Examples are patterns to adapt: never copy their numbers.

## ISO/IEC 25010:2023 in brief

- Second edition, 2023-11. Together with ISO/IEC 25002 (quality models overview and usage) and
  ISO/IEC 25019 (quality-in-use model) it replaces ISO/IEC 25010:2011.
- Renamed: usability → interaction capability (IC); portability → flexibility (FLEX).
- New characteristic: safety (SAFE). New sub-characteristics: inclusivity and self-descriptiveness
  (IC), resistance (SEC), scalability (FLEX).
- Replaced: user interface aesthetics → user engagement; maturity → faultlessness; accessibility
  is split into inclusivity and user assistance.
- Quality in use (2011: effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, freedom from risk, context
  coverage) is now ISO/IEC 25019:2023 (beneficialness, freedom from risk, acceptability). It judges
  outcomes for stakeholders in a context of use: such outcomes are scenarios in
  `{{vars.requirementsFile}}`, not catalog rows.
- Documents written with 2011 terms map through the lines above.

## Sub-characteristics and typical measures

| Code | Sub-characteristic | Means | Typical measure |
|---|---|---|---|
| FUNC | Functional completeness | the functions cover the specified tasks and objectives | rarely a row: Dev QA's ID mapping checks it |
| FUNC | Functional correctness | results are correct with the needed precision | decimal places, rounding mode, units, time zone rule; 0 differences against a reference data set |
| FUNC | Functional appropriateness | the functions help complete the task without needless steps | steps per task; rarely a row |
| PERF | Time behaviour | response time and throughput | p95 or p99 at a stated load; sustained throughput; a batch done by a deadline |
| PERF | Resource utilization | amounts and types of resources used, energy included | peak memory per job; payload size; battery drain per hour |
| PERF | Capacity | maximum limits of a parameter | largest file or data set, concurrent users, requests per second at which time targets still hold |
| COMP | Co-existence | performs well in a shared environment without harming other products | connections, quota or host share used at peak |
| COMP | Interoperability | exchanges information with other systems and uses it | formats and protocol versions; contract tests per consumer or provider |
| IC | Appropriateness recognizability | users can tell whether it fits their needs | the entry point is visible from a named place |
| IC | Learnability | users learn the functions within a set time | a first task done without help within N minutes |
| IC | Operability | easy to operate and control | every action reachable by keyboard; steps per task |
| IC | User error protection | prevents operation errors | destructive actions confirmed or undoable for N seconds; invalid input rejected before submit |
| IC | User engagement | presents functions and information in an inviting way | rarely a row; strict rows only under `ui-design` |
| IC | Inclusivity | usable by people of different backgrounds, ages, abilities, cultures, languages | locales supported; WCAG 2.2 AA |
| IC | User assistance | people with the widest range of capabilities reach their goals, with assistive technology too | every state change announced to screen readers |
| IC | Self-descriptiveness | shows its capabilities and use without extra help | every error names the cause and the next action; a CLI help lists every option |
| REL | Faultlessness | performs without fault in normal operation (formerly maturity) | failed jobs per 1 000; 0 unhandled errors on documented inputs |
| REL | Availability | operational and accessible when needed | 99.9 % per month, with the measurement window |
| REL | Fault tolerance | works as intended despite hardware or software faults | outcome when a named dependency is down or slow; 0 lost writes |
| REL | Recoverability | restores data and the desired state after a failure | RPO, RTO; a long job resumes from its last checkpoint |
| SEC | Confidentiality | data accessible only to those authorized | 0 rows of another tenant; encrypted in transit and at rest |
| SEC | Integrity | prevents unauthorized change or deletion of data or programs | every write authorized on the server side; input validated at the boundary |
| SEC | Non-repudiation | actions or events can be proven to have happened | an append-only, signed record of each approval |
| SEC | Accountability | actions can be traced to a unique entity | an audit entry with actor, action and time per administrative change |
| SEC | Authenticity | the identity of a subject or resource can be proven | multi-factor sign-in for administrators; incoming webhook signatures verified |
| SEC | Resistance | keeps operating while under attack | at most 10 sign-in attempts per minute per account; time targets hold under a request flood |
| MAINT | Modularity | discrete components; a change to one barely affects others | dependency rules between capabilities (static) |
| MAINT | Reusability | an asset serves more than one system or helps build others | only when reuse is a stated goal |
| MAINT | Analysability | impact of a change, cause of a failure and parts to change are found quickly | every failure traceable to one request or job |
| MAINT | Modifiability | changes without new defects or loss of quality | a named likely change touches one capability (change scenario) |
| MAINT | Testability | test criteria can be set and tests run against them | external services replaceable by fakes; time and randomness controllable |
| FLEX | Adaptability | adapts to other hardware, software, operational or usage environments | supported platforms, browsers, viewports |
| FLEX | Scalability | handles growing or shrinking workloads; adapts capacity to variability | N times today's peak by adding instances, without code changes |
| FLEX | Installability | installs and uninstalls successfully in a specified environment | one command on a clean host within N minutes; nothing left after removal |
| FLEX | Replaceability | can take the place of another product with the same purpose in the same environment | a provider switch changes no caller; data exportable in an open format |
| SAFE | Operational constraint | keeps operation within safe limits when a hazard arises | a hard daily limit on transfers |
| SAFE | Risk identification | recognizes event sequences that expose people, property or the environment to unacceptable risk | the rules that flag such a sequence |
| SAFE | Fail safe | switches to a safe mode or state on failure | deny by default when a check cannot run |
| SAFE | Hazard warning | warns of unacceptable risk early enough to react | a warning before a bulk action affecting more than N customers |
| SAFE | Safe integration | stays safe during and after integration with other components | safety checks run again when a component is replaced |

## Notes per code

- **FUNC**: the behavior itself lives in `{{vars.requirementsFile}}`; write only precision a
  scenario cannot carry (money, units, rounding, time zones).
- **IC** holds section 5, UI appearance, and applies to CLIs. In 25010, operability means end users
  operating the product; concerns of operations staff go to OBS and DEPLOY.
- **SEC** integrity and **MAINT** testability apply to every change (Applicable or Baseline).
- **SAFE** (new in 2023): harm to life, health, property or the environment; in business software
  mostly money movement, health data, devices and irreversible bulk actions.

## Example requirements (Requirement · Measure · Verify by)

| ID | Example |
|---|---|
| PAY-FUNC-1 | Amounts shall be exact decimals with 2 places, rounded half-even · 0 differences against the reference set · test |
| ORD-PERF-1 | When a user exports up to 100 000 orders, the download shall start · p95 ≤ 2 s to first byte · bench |
| ORD-COMP-1 | The order-placed event shall stay readable by consumers of the previous version · contract test passes · test |
| ORD-IC-2 | When a user deletes an export, the deletion shall be undoable · for 30 s · test |
| ORD-REL-1 | If the payment provider does not answer within 10 s, the order shall stay pending and be retried · 0 lost orders · test |
| ORD-SEC-1 | An export shall contain only orders of the requester's tenant · 0 foreign rows · test |
| ORD-MAINT-1 | When an export format is added, the change shall touch only the export capability and its tests · 0 other capabilities changed · inspect |
| ORD-FLEX-1 | The order store shall absorb 3 times today's peak write rate by adding instances · no code change · bench |
| PAY-SAFE-1 | A bulk payout above 10 000 EUR shall need a second approver · yes/no · test |

## Extra codes

| Code | Covers | Typical measures | Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| OBS | logs, metrics, traces, alerts | questions operators must answer; alert threshold and window | alert when export failures exceed 1 % for 5 min (ops) |
| DEPLOY | release, rollback, feature flags | rollback time; deploy without downtime; switch-off without a deployment | rollback within 5 min without data loss (inspect) |
| CICD | pipeline gates | checks that block merge; pipeline duration | tests, type check and dependency audit block merge (inspect) |
| COST | money to run the feature | monthly ceiling at expected load; cost per business event | ≤ 50 USD per month at 2 000 orders per day (ops) |
| PRIV | personal data: purpose, minimization, retention, erasure | retention and its start event; fields never logged | personal data deleted within 30 days of account closure (test) |
| DCONS | consistency and durability across stores, caches, asynchronous flows | staleness bound; atomicity; delivery guarantee | search includes an order within 60 s of a change (test) |
| COMPL | regulated data or markets | the rule and the regulation it comes from | card numbers are never stored (static) |
| DX | developer experience | clone to green tests; local run with faked services | one command from a fresh clone to green tests, ≤ 10 min (inspect) |

## SEI names at sub-characteristic level

The catalog maps SEI names to codes. Where the sub-characteristic matters: availability → REL
availability, fault tolerance, recoverability; energy efficiency → PERF resource utilization;
integrability → COMP interoperability, MAINT modularity; modifiability → MAINT modifiability and
modularity, FLEX adaptability; testability → MAINT testability. SEI's other attributes:
portability → FLEX adaptability and installability; mobility → FLEX adaptability, PERF resource
utilization; monitorability → OBS.
